<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#monastery">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Monastery</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as vihara</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A monastery is a building or complex of buildings housing monastics, monks or nuns, whether living in communities or alone as hermits, with a place reserved for prayer such as a chapel, church or temple. The word comes from the Greek monasterion, from monazein, 'to live alone'. Buddhist monasteries, called vihara, grew from the retreat monks and nuns kept during the South Asian wet season. According to tradition, Christian monasticism began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anthony-the-great">Anthony the Great</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachomius-the-great">Pachomius the Great</a> developed communal life under one roof; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedict-of-nursia">Benedict of Nursia</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cassino">Monte Cassino</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 529, the seed of Roman Catholic monasticism.</p></div>
